fix.summary:
I fixed every verified finding (B1-B10 and m1-m5) at its root cause in internal/config/profile, and each fix has a test that fails on the pre-round code. Every block collection is now parsed entry by entry (defaultSplitAt = 1), so goccy only parses keys and non-block values, each in a fresh context. That makes results independent of how many siblings a document has. A trailing '?' entry with no ':' also gets its null at the right position. The token pass now reads values and positions from the source, through a scalarFix table that the converter uses:
- quoted scalars (quoted.go): YAML 1.2 escapes, folding and escaped line breaks;
- block scalars (literal.go): indentation, chomping and folding as PyYAML and libyaml read them;
- plain scalars over several lines (plain.go): folding, plus comment and ': ' errors at the offending text.
Plain tokens goccy scans out of other nodes are now refused as RZ-CFG-001, RZ-CFG-003 or RZ-CFG-004, and the tag column is fixed. Parse appends a final line break when the text lacks one, following the YAML Test Suite convention. With that, suite case L24T/01 passes and I removed it from expected-failures.txt. Two cost bounds for one flow collection stop goccy's quadratic behavior: a path-byte charge (maxFlowPath) and a null-insertion move bound (maxFlowNullShift). The default token limit drops from 1,000,000 to 400,000, which keeps the densest shapes under 190 MiB peak heap growth, inside the 256 MiB budget. Every check listed below is green, and nothing outside internal/config/profile/ and test/fixtures/yaml-test-suite/ changed.

lensVerdicts.summary:
WP-33 is not ready to land: three cost bounds can still be broken, all measured, and no accepted tree I found is deeper than MaxDepth. Probes ran through go test -overlay with Parse and default Options. The probe file is outside the repository: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-ruralz/758d7ca3-81cf-5fbc-a967-12d97bce3d6b/scratchpad/wp33r2-depthcost-x7q/dc_probe_test.go. Run it with `go test -overlay <dir>/overlay.json -count=1 -v -run '^TestDCProbe$' ./internal/config/profile/`. No repository file was changed.

The post-parse depth guard (converter.enter, splitter.frame) runs on every path that builds a tree: split frames, goccy-parsed leaves and goccy's whole parse. It runs after goccy has parsed a value, though. On the split path the cost before it is bounded: block structure, paths and inserted nulls are linear. These shapes were all linear: many documents, multi-line plain, quoted and block scalars, comments, flow pairs, explicit keys. The byte estimate never fell below goccy's token count on a set of indicator-dense units. Two fallbacks still hand block structure to goccy whole, and there the guard comes too late.

1. A '...' line at column 1 followed by text (`...x: 1`, `... x: 1`). goccy ends the document there; splitDocuments does not. bodyRange then fails and parseSegment parses the whole document with goccy. Results:
   - accepted trees that differ from YAML 1.2: `a: 1\nb: 2\n...x: 1` becomes two documents, and goccy's misnesting comes back, up to depth 60 in 208 bytes;
   - 988,898 bytes accepted in 22.2 s at a 346 MiB peak;
   - 96,860 bytes accepted at a 1,039 MiB peak;
   - 50,008 bytes reach an 810 MiB peak before the depth guard fails the document.

2. Inside one entry, a block collection followed by a line at an intermediate column (`a:\n  k: v ... \n c: 2`). rangeNode's covers check fails and goccy parses the whole range before refusing it:
   - 875,009 bytes: 36.1 s, 360 MiB;
   - 90,009 bytes: 811 MiB;
   - 95,921 bytes: 1,037 MiB.

Both paths reopen round-1 blockers 1 and 2, so those fixes are incomplete.

3. A third blocker is in the flow-collection bound itself. flowcost misses goccy's second token-insertion site, the default value goccy inserts after a scalar core tag before ',' or ':'. Valid YAML is accepted slowly: `[!!str , ...]` at 693,007 bytes takes 9.8 s, and a flow mapping of tagged empty values at 948,900 bytes takes 6.2 s. The round-1 null fix is incomplete here too.

There is also one major finding outside this lens: `a:\n  -\n  b: 1` is accepted as {a:[{b:1}]}, while YAML 1.2 rejects it. One minor finding: the 2 s per-input bound is asserted for only 4 test rows and not at all in FuzzLoadYAML.

